sections.s2_technical:
The central mistake is treating a migration as a single deploy: change the schema, flip the code, run a big backfill, done. At hundreds of millions of rows that approach either requires downtime or breaks in production. The disciplined approach separates four concerns that can each fail independently and each be verified independently.

The first phase is schema compatibility. You introduce the new representation additively — a new column, a new table, a new index — without removing or renaming anything the current application depends on. On large tables this itself must be online: adding a nullable column or a new table is cheap, but adding a non-null column with a default, or building an index, must be done with online, non-blocking DDL so you do not lock the table against live traffic. The invariant for this phase is that the old application, unchanged, continues to work perfectly.

```sql
-- Phase 1: additive only. Old app never references new_balance_minor, so it is unaffected.
ALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN balance_minor BIGINT NULL;  -- store rupees as paise (integer)
-- Build the index CONCURRENTLY so it does not lock the table against production writes.
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_accounts_balance_minor ON accounts(balance_minor);
```

The second phase is transitional application code. You deploy code that writes to both the old and new representations on every change — dual writes — so that from this point forward, new data is correct in the new representation automatically. Reads still come from the old representation. This phase is reversible: if something is wrong, you stop dual-writing and the old path is untouched.

```java
// Phase 2: dual-write. Every update keeps old and new in sync going forward.
void updateBalance(Account a, BigDecimal newBalance) {
    a.setBalance(newBalance);                              // old representation (decimal)
    a.setBalanceMinor(newBalance.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()); // new representation (paise)
    repo.save(a);
}
```

The third phase is the backfill of historical data, and this is where production reality bites. You cannot run one giant UPDATE over hundreds of millions of rows — it holds locks, bloats the transaction log, and starves live queries. Instead you backfill in bounded batches, say a few thousand rows at a time, with a short pause between batches, with throttling that backs off when production latency rises, with observability on progress and error rate, and crucially with restartability so a crash resumes from the last committed batch rather than starting over. You only backfill rows the dual-write has not already fixed.

```java
// Phase 3: bounded, throttled, restartable backfill
long lastId = checkpoint.load();                 // resume from here after any crash
while (true) {
    List<Account> batch = repo.findBatchAfter(lastId, 5000);   // bounded
    if (batch.isEmpty()) break;
    batch.forEach(a -> a.setBalanceMinor(a.getBalance().movePointRight(2).longValueExact()));
    repo.saveAll(batch);
    lastId = batch.get(batch.size() - 1).getId();
    checkpoint.save(lastId);                      // restartable
    if (productionLatencyHigh()) sleep(throttleMs());   // throttle under load
}
```

The fourth phase is continuous verification and staged cutover. While the backfill runs and after it completes, a verifier samples rows and checks that the old and new representations agree; any mismatch is logged and investigated before proceeding. Only when verification is clean do you move reads to the new representation, observe for a while, then move writes exclusively to the new one, and finally — in a completely separate, later deployment — remove the old column in the contract phase. Each step is independently reversible until the contract.

The reason staging lies is resource contention. In staging, the backfill has the database to itself: empty buffer cache competition, no lock contention, no replication lag, idle disks. In production the same backfill competes with live reads and writes for CPU, disk I/O, the buffer pool, row locks, and replication bandwidth. A backfill that finishes in an hour in staging can, in production, cause replica lag, lock timeouts for customer transactions, and I/O saturation — unless it is throttled and batched. The senior-level nuance is that the throttle must be adaptive, driven by live signals like production query latency and replication lag, and the whole migration must be pausable and resumable so you can stop it the instant it hurts customers and continue later.

